# Copy to .env and fill in. .env is gitignored; this file is not.
#
# Nothing here is optional except NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL. The app fails with a
# readable message rather than a stack trace when one is missing, but it fails.

# --------------------------------------------------------------------- Claude

# Powers the FAQ generator, the dashboard generator, and the content plan.
# Get one at https://console.anthropic.com/settings/keys
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=sk-ant-your-key-here

# ------------------------------------------------- Answer engines (tracking)
#
# The three engines citation tracking asks. Each is a separate vendor with a
# separate bill, and all three are OPTIONAL — a missing key means that engine
# reports "not configured" and the run continues with the others. Hearing back
# from two of three is a partial answer worth keeping; losing the whole run
# because one key expired is not.
#
# ⚠️ THESE ARE THE APIs, NOT THE CONSUMER APPS. We query the OpenAI API with its
# web search tool and the Gemini API with Google Search grounding — not
# chatgpt.com or gemini.google.com. Different system prompt, different
# retrieval, no personalisation. The dashboard says so on the page; do not let
# anyone describe it as a recording of a real chat.
#
# ⚠️ GOOGLE AI OVERVIEWS IS ABSENT AND CANNOT BE ADDED WITH A KEY. It has no
# API at all — tools that report on it collect Google result pages at scale.
# That is why ENGINES names Gemini instead: an engine we cannot ask would show
# a permanent zero, which reads as "you are never cited there" rather than "we
# never looked".
#
# Cost note: Gemini bills grounded queries separately from tokens and OpenAI
# bills the web search, so a tracking run costs real money per question per
# engine. TRACKING_RATE_LIMIT in lib/rate-limit.ts is what bounds it today.

# https://www.perplexity.ai/settings/api — the cheapest of the three, and the
# one whose citations are first-class rather than dug out of the response.
PERPLEXITY_API_KEY=pplx-your-key-here

# https://platform.openai.com/api-keys
OPENAI_API_KEY=sk-your-key-here

# https://aistudio.google.com/apikey
GEMINI_API_KEY=your-key-here

# ------------------------------------------------------------------- Supabase

# Project Settings → API, in the Supabase dashboard.
#
# The anon key is meant to reach the browser — that is why it is NEXT_PUBLIC_.
# What keeps it safe is row-level security, not secrecy, so if hiding it ever
# feels necessary the thing that actually needs fixing is a missing policy.
# See supabase/migrations/0001_auth_profiles_and_sites.sql.
# Real values belong in .env, which is gitignored. This file is committed, so
# it carries the shape and nothing else — a template holding one project's
# credentials gets copied into other environments by whoever reads it next.
#
# The key is the "publishable" one and starts sb_publishable_ (note the s —
# it is easy to lose a leading character when copying).
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=https://your-project.supabase.co
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=sb_publishable_your-key-here

# ⚠️ SERVICE ROLE — bypasses row-level security entirely.
#
# Required for Stripe fulfilment: `sites.get_cited_at` and
# `profiles.subscription` have no UPDATE grant for signed-in users (so a
# browser can never grant itself a paid tier), which means the webhook needs a
# credential that outranks those grants. This is that credential.
#
# It is total read/write access to every row of every table for every account.
# Never give it a NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix — that inlines a value into the browser
# bundle. Never import lib/supabase/admin.ts from a component.
# Project Settings → API → service_role.
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=your-service-role-key

# --------------------------------------------------------------------- Stripe

# sk_test_… locally, sk_live_… in production.
#
# ⚠️ TEST AND LIVE ARE SEPARATE NAMESPACES. Not just the key: prices, customers,
# webhook endpoints and their signing secrets, and Customer Portal activation
# all exist twice and share nothing. An id from one does not resolve in the
# other — Stripe answers "No such price" / "No such customer", which reads like
# a typo rather than a mode mismatch. The checkout routes now say so explicitly
# when they see `resource_missing`.
#
# ⚠️ AND ONE THAT SURVIVES THE SWITCH: profiles.stripe_customer_id is reused
# rather than recreated, so a customer id created in test mode stays in the
# database and poisons that account's live checkout forever. Before the first
# live purchase:
#   update public.profiles set stripe_customer_id = null
#     where stripe_customer_id is not null;
# The column repopulates itself on the next purchase.
STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=sk_test_your-key

# The webhook signing secret. NOT the API key, and NOT the same value in both
# places: locally it is printed by `stripe listen`, in production it comes from
# the webhook endpoint you register in the Stripe dashboard.
#
#   stripe listen --api-key "$(grep '^STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=' .env | cut -d= -f2-)" \
#     --forward-to localhost:3000/api/stripe/webhook
#
# --api-key is worth the noise: without it the CLI uses its own stored login,
# which expires independently of everything here and may point at a different
# account entirely. Passing the key from .env guarantees the listener and the
# app are looking at the same Stripe.
#
# The local secret CHANGES every time `stripe listen` restarts. Deploying a
# stale one — or the local one to production — gives 400s on every delivery, so
# payments succeed in Stripe and unlock nothing. A MISSING one is now a 500
# rather than a 400, so the two are distinguishable in Stripe's dashboard.
STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=whsec_your-secret

# Price ids (price_…), not product ids (prod_…). Create three prices in the
# Stripe dashboard and paste their ids here. Keeping them in env rather than in
# code means an amount can change without a deploy, and test and live modes can
# differ without a branch.
#
#   Get Cited    one-time    — charged per site
#   Stay Cited   recurring monthly  \ the pricing page offers both,
#   Stay Cited   recurring yearly   / so both must exist
#
# ⚠️ Stripe charges what ITS price says. The figures on the pricing page are
# separate copy — keeping the two in agreement is a manual job.
STRIPE_PRICE_GET_CITED=price_...
STRIPE_PRICE_STAY_CITED_MONTHLY=price_...
STRIPE_PRICE_STAY_CITED_ANNUAL=price_...

# ------------------------------------------------------- Coupons / promo codes
#
# No env var, which is exactly why this is written down: nothing in the repo
# names a single code. Get Cited checkout sets allow_promotion_codes, so the
# field is always on its Stripe page, and the codes live entirely in Stripe.
# Stay Cited does not take codes — see lib/stripe/checkout.ts for why.
#
# TWO objects, and confusing them is the usual first mistake:
#
#   COUPON          the discount itself      e.g. 50% off, or $40 off
#   PROMOTION CODE  the string a customer types, attached to a coupon  e.g. LAUNCH50
#
# A coupon on its own cannot be redeemed at checkout. Stripe dashboard →
# Product catalogue → Coupons → create one, then "Add promotion code" to it.
#
# ⚠️ PER MODE, like prices. Codes created in test mode do not exist in live.
# ⚠️ A coupon's duration (once / repeating / forever) is meaningless against a
#    ONE-TIME price — there is only ever one payment. It matters for
#    subscriptions, which is the reason Stay Cited does not offer codes yet.
# ⚠️ A 100%-OFF code makes payment_status 'no_payment_required', NOT 'paid'.
#    lib/stripe/fulfil.ts grants on it deliberately. Read the warning there
#    before "tidying" that check.
# ⚠️ Give any public code a redemption limit AND an expiry. A code that reaches
#    a deals site without a cap is unbounded free product, and refunding does
#    not revoke access today.

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------- Email
#
# ONE Resend account, TWO separate paths, and they are configured in different
# places. Getting them confused is the usual way this ends up half-working:
#
#   1. SUPABASE'S AUTH MAIL — confirmation and password reset.
#      Sent by Supabase over Resend's SMTP. NO code, NO env var here.
#      Supabase → Authentication → SMTP Settings:
#        host smtp.resend.com   port 465   username resend
#        password = your Resend API key
#      Then Authentication → Rate Limits: RAISE the email limit. It stays low
#      even after SMTP is configured, and the built-in default is low enough to
#      lock a real customer out — an account cannot be used until it is
#      confirmed, so a throttled email is a customer who can never get in.
#
#   2. OUR OWN MAIL — the welcome and "you're set up" emails.
#      Sent by this app over Resend's REST API. That is what these two are for.
#
# ⚠️ Resend will not deliver to arbitrary addresses until the sending domain is
# verified (Resend → Domains → add faqflo.com, then its SPF/DKIM records).
# Until then it only accepts your own account address, so testing is limited.
RESEND_API_KEY=re_your-key
#
# ⚠️ THE TWO ADDRESSES BELOW ARE ON DIFFERENT DOMAINS, DELIBERATELY.
#
#   EMAIL_FROM      the domain verified in Resend. Verifying a SUBDOMAIN
#                   (send.yourdomain.com) rather than the root is the point:
#                   bulk mail then cannot damage the reputation of the domain
#                   the business runs on. It is send-only and has no MX record.
#                   A `from` outside the verified domain is rejected outright.
#
#   EMAIL_REPLY_TO  a real mailbox on the ROOT domain. Both templates say
#                   "reply to this email" — without this, that reply goes to
#                   the send-only subdomain and is simply lost. Optional in
#                   code, but leaving it empty makes the copy a lie.
EMAIL_FROM=FaqFlo <hello@send.yourdomain.com>
EMAIL_REPLY_TO=hello@yourdomain.com

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ Deployment

# Optional. The canonical origin, used to build the links inside confirmation
# and password-reset emails, and the OAuth redirect back from Google.
#
# Leave it unset locally and in previews: the app reads the request headers
# instead, which is what makes a preview deployment link back to itself rather
# than to production. Set it in production so the links are always canonical
# even behind a proxy.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=https://www.faqflo.com

# ---------------------------------------------------- Google sign-in (set up)
#
# No env vars — the credentials live in Supabase, not here. Once, per project:
#
#   1. Google Cloud Console → APIs & Services → Credentials
#      → Create OAuth client ID → Web application.
#   2. Authorised redirect URI: the one shown on Supabase's Google provider
#      page (https://<project>.supabase.co/auth/v1/callback).
#      NOT this app's /auth/callback — that is where Supabase sends people
#      afterwards, and it is configured separately.
#   3. Paste the client ID and secret into Supabase → Authentication →
#      Providers → Google, and enable it.
#   4. Supabase → Authentication → URL Configuration → Redirect URLs:
#      add http://localhost:3000/** and your production origin.

# ------------------------------------------------------ Deploying to production
#
# Half of this checklist lives in dashboards rather than in this repo, which is
# why it is written down here: two of the settings break sign-up SILENTLY. The
# build passes, the marketing pages work, and only the auth routes are dead —
# so it reads as a code bug and gets debugged in the wrong place.
#
#   1. VERCEL → Settings → Environment Variables. Add the two Supabase values
#      above to BOTH Production and Preview.
#
#      ⚠️ NEXT_PUBLIC_* is inlined at BUILD time, not read at runtime. Adding
#      them after a deploy changes nothing — that bundle already contains
#      `undefined`. Set them first, or redeploy afterwards.
#
#   2. SUPABASE → Authentication → URL Configuration → Site URL.
#      Set it to your production origin. It defaults to http://localhost:3000,
#      and it is what confirmation and password-reset emails fall back to — so
#      leaving it means production emails carry localhost links that cannot
#      work for anyone.
#
#   3. SUPABASE → Authentication → URL Configuration → Redirect URLs.
#      Keep localhost and add:
#        https://<your-domain>/**
#        https://*-<your-vercel-team-slug>.vercel.app/**    (preview deploys)
#
#      Use /** and not /* — a single star does not match nested paths, and
#      /auth/callback is nested. A missing entry fails at the last hop of
#      Google sign-in, AFTER the consent screen, which looks like our bug.
#
#   4. NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL: set on Production only; leave it unset on Preview
#      so each preview links back to itself. See lib/auth/origin.ts.
#
#   5. SUPABASE → Authentication → Emails → SMTP Settings.
#      The built-in mailer is a development service and is rate-limited. Since
#      an account cannot be used until its email is confirmed, a throttled
#      email is a customer who can never get in. Point it at a real provider
#      before launch. Google sign-in is unaffected.
#
#   6. Run every migration in supabase/migrations/ against the project the
#      deployment points at — they are not applied automatically.
#
#   7. STRIPE → Workbench → Webhooks. Register
#      https://<your-domain>/api/stripe/webhook and subscribe to exactly:
#        checkout.session.completed
#        checkout.session.async_payment_succeeded
#        customer.subscription.created
#        customer.subscription.updated
#        customer.subscription.deleted
#        charge.refunded              \ revoke Get Cited — see the handler in
#        charge.dispute.created       / app/api/stripe/webhook/route.ts
#
#      ⚠️ An endpoint not subscribed to the last two simply never hears about a
#      refund. Nothing errors: refunds just silently stop revoking access, and
#      it looks like a bug in the handler rather than a missing subscription.
#      Applies to BOTH endpoints — the live one and the local `stripe listen`
#      (which forwards everything by default, so local is usually fine).
#
#      Its signing secret is DIFFERENT from the local `stripe listen` one —
#      put the production value in STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET on Vercel.
#
#   8. STRIPE → Settings → Billing → Customer portal. Activate it, or
#      "Manage billing" returns an error rather than a portal.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------- Switching to LIVE
#
# Steps 7 and 8 are per-MODE, not per-project: doing them in test mode does
# nothing for live. Going live means creating a second set of everything.
#
#   a. STRIPE → activate the account (business details, bank account). Live
#      charges are impossible until this is approved. Start it first; it is the
#      only step with someone else in the loop.
#
#   b. Create the three prices AGAIN in live mode. They get new price ids.
#      They must match components/marketing/pricing-teaser.tsx exactly —
#      Stripe charges what its price says and the page is only copy.
#
#   c. Register the webhook AGAIN in live mode (step 7's five events). Its
#      signing secret differs from both the test endpoint's and `stripe listen`'s.
#
#   d. Activate the Customer Portal AGAIN in live mode.
#
#   e. ⚠️ CLEAR THE TEST-MODE CUSTOMER IDS before the first live purchase:
#        update public.profiles set stripe_customer_id = null
#          where stripe_customer_id is not null;
#      Otherwise the first live checkout hands a cus_… that only exists in test
#      mode to a live API call and fails with "No such customer" — for that
#      account, every time, until the column is cleared. This bites hardest
#      when production and local share one Supabase project, which is the
#      normal setup for a single-project app.
#
#   f. Vercel → Production: swap STRIPE_SECRET_KEY and STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET,
#      and all THREE STRIPE_PRICE_* to the live ids. Then REDEPLOY.
#
#      ⚠️ The price vars are easy to miss in an audit: lib/stripe/products.ts
#      reads them as process.env[name] with a computed name, so grepping for
#      process.env.STRIPE_PRICE_ finds nothing.
#
#   g. Verify with ONE real card, then refund it — and note that refunding does
#      NOT revoke the entitlement today, so clear sites.get_cited_at by hand.
